  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nMid/upper troughing remains across the Upper Midwest with a 45+\nknot H5 jet extending from southern Saskatchewan into\nnorthwestern Iowa. This is pushing a cold front southeastward \ninto southern Minnesota, NW Iowa, and NE Nebraska, and has \nhelped initiate convection across northern Iowa this afternoon.\nConvection may continue to develop along the southwest flank of\nthe current convection, and move south southeastward toward NE\nMissouri. Most CAMs dissipate storms before they reach the\nMissouri border, but some (namely the NAM Nest) want to bring a\ncluster of storms or even an MCS into our CWA across northern\nand NE Missouri after 10 pm tonight. SPC brings a slight risk\nfor severe storms all the way to the Missouri/Iowa border, with\nthe marginal risk extending southward to roughly the US Highway\n36 corridor. \n\nBy tomorrow, a 598 dam H5 high sets up over the Four Corners,\nwith increasing mid levels heights creeping in over our CWA from\nthe west. High temperatures tomorrow should be a couple/few\ndegrees warmer, with afternoon heat indices reaching or\nexceeding 100 degrees for areas long and south of the Interstate\n70 corridor. While our PoPs for tomorrow afternoon at this time\nare below 10%, cannot completely rule out a small complex of\nshowers/storms developing over eastern Nebraska and moving into\nNW Missouri. There will be a chance for an MCS to develop over\nIowa late Saturday night as the southerly low level jet ramps\nup, with this potentially moving into northern Missouri. Both \nof these potential scenarios have prompted SPC to maintain a \nmarginal risk for severe storms for most of the CWA for tomorrow\nand tomorrow night. The gradual warming trend is expected to \ncontinue into Sunday and into early next week, with afternoon \nhighs forecast to reach the mid 90s and afternoon heat indices \napproaching the mid 100s (potentially prompting the issuance of \na heat advisory, stay tuned for that!). Cooler temperatures and\nunsettled weather are probable for the middle to end of next\nweek.",
